2821 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(While headlines fixated on economic uncertainty, the\nworld\342\200\231s wealthiest buyers were quietly doing what they\342\200\231ve\nalways done\342\200\224buying. The enduring power of luxury real\nestate, particularly properties priced at US$10 million\nand\302\240US$20 million and above, exceeded expectations in\n20251 and is poised to continue that trajectory into 2026,\naccording to Philip A. White Jr., president and CEO,\nSotheby\342\200\231s International Realty.\n\342\200\234Even after tumultuous local elections and amid high\ninterest rates, as well as proposed tax measures, we\342\200\231re still\nseeing strong property markets in New York City, across\nthe U.S. and around the world,\342\200\235 White says. \342\200\234Despite\nmacroeconomic and geopolitical headlines, the\302\240affluent\nremain engaged and confident in the luxury housing\nmarket. They are deliberate but not deterred. Demand for\nbest-in-class properties continues to demonstrate that\nprime real estate holds its position as a\302\240trusted asset and\na powerful expression of lifestyle and\302\240legacy.\342\200\235\nLuxury real estate investments benefit from similar\noptimism about finances and macroeconomics. \342\200\234There\342\200\231s\na\302\240high correlation between stock market performance\nand\302\240the luxury housing market,\342\200\235 says Selma Hepp, chief\neconomist at Cotality, a property data analytics company.\nThe S&P 500 index, tracking the top companies on U.S.\nstock exchanges, rose approximately 80% from early\n2023 through 2025, with many investors expecting further\ngains, according to a January 2026 analysis by The Wall\nStreet Journal.2\n\342\200\234There\342\200\231s been a lot of wealth creation because of\nthe\302\240stock market,\342\200\235 Hepp says. \342\200\234The increase in aggregate\nwealth in the top 10% of Americans over the past five\nyears is approximately US$40 trillion compared to just\nUS$3 trillion for the bottom 50% of income brackets.\342\200\235\nThis is backed up by data released by the U.S. Federal\nReserve in January 2026, which showed a steep rise in\nthe net worth of the top 1% of wealthy Americans, totaling\nUS$54 trillion by the\302\240third quarter of 2025.3 Hepp points\nout that, in addition to stock market performance, wealth)/P 55 0 R/K[3570 0 R]/Pg 16 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

