2936 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(\342\200\234It\342\200\231s more of a confidence issue\nthan a\302\240financial reality that\342\200\231s holding\nback homebuyers a little on the upper\nend,\342\200\235 Holleran says.\nStill, Boston and Cambridge\nconsistently attract deep-pocketed\nhomebuyers from the financial services\nsector and technology and life sciences\nfields, she adds. For those that prefer to live\nin Cambridge, the rarity of a fully renovated\nhistoric home keeps the market tight.\nDallas, Texas\nThe city\342\200\231s housing market has been\nstable, which bodes well for the rest of\n2026, says\302\240Rachel Finkbohner, global\nreal estate advisor, Briggs Freeman\nSotheby\342\200\231s International Realty.\n\342\200\234We do 70% of our business\nbetween January and June, and typically\nhave 30-day closings, so things move fast,\342\200\235\nFinkbohner says. \342\200\234Our inventory in Dallas\nis really tight, particularly in neighborhoods\nwith a range of educational resources,\nwhere homes are priced from\nUS$2.5 million to US$6 million.\342\200\235\nThe Dallas market is \342\200\234hyper-obsessed\342\200\235\nwith location, according to Finkbohner,\nwith some homebuyers only wanting to\nlive on one or two specific streets.\n\342\200\234Most of our homebuyers are locals,\nso they know where they want to be,\342\200\235 she\nsays. \342\200\234But more companies are moving to\nDallas, too, so the number of out-of-market)/P 55 0 R/K[3652 0 R]/Pg 27 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

2937 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(homebuyers is starting to increase in\nthe city compared to the rest of the state,\nwith the top leadership of these companies\nbuying homes in high-end neighborhoods.\342\200\235\nWhile in other U.S. markets people\nhave tended to stay in their homes for\nlonger in recent years, in Dallas people\noften look to upsize, upgrade for style or\nshift to a more desirable neighborhood\nafter a few years in their home, according\nto Finkbohner. \342\200\234In neighborhoods like\nUniversity Park, some developers are\nbuilding homes in the US$7 million to\nUS$12 million range,\342\200\235 she explains.\n\342\200\234Otherwise, most of the new construction\nis custom homes built after an existing\nhome is torn down.\342\200\235\nFinkbohner anticipates a strong\nmarket throughout 2026 but hopes\nfor more inventory, particularly in the\nUS$2.5\302\240million to US$4 million range\nto\302\240increase transaction volume.\nSan Francisco,\nCalifornia\nArtificial intelligence \(AI\) has triggered\na\302\240renaissance for the Bay Area, according\nto Alex Hachiya, senior global real estate\nadvisor, Sotheby\342\200\231s International Realty -\nSan Francisco Brokerage.\n\342\200\234San Francisco thrives on innovation\nand new technology, and now the AI boom\nis real,\342\200\235 Hachiya says. \342\200\234Wealth flooded\nback into the city in 2025, and we saw)/P 55 0 R/K[3653 0 R]/Pg 27 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

2938 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(84% more sales of homes priced\nat US$7.5 million and up that year,\ncompared to 2024.\342\200\235\nThat sales trajectory boosted\nconfidence at every level in the city\ngoing into 2026, assisted by new political\nleadership restoring San Francisco and\nits\302\240reputation, he says.\n\342\200\234It\342\200\231s been a U-turn, with people\nmoving back here from New York, New\nYork, Austin, Texas, Washington, D.C.\nand elsewhere,\342\200\235 Hachiya continues. \342\200\234San\nFrancisco has always been a trophy city\nfor international homebuyers, although we\nhaven\342\200\231t seen as many foreign homebuyers\nin the past few years.\342\200\235\nHomebuyers in their 20s and 30s\nwho work in tech, are entrepreneurs or\nhave family wealth buy in the city at first\nand then move to nearby suburbs when\nthey have children, Hachiya notes.\n\342\200\234When they\342\200\231re a little older and their\nkids go to college, those homebuyers\npurchase a weekend place in the city,\342\200\235 he\nsays. \342\200\234We also see demand from empty-\nnesters and older people who want to buy\na\302\240new place in a building with an elevator\nas part of their retirement plan. We see\nsome grandparents buying places in the\narea so they can be closer to their kids\nand grandkids, too.\342\200\235\nAll homebuyers, but especially\nyounger ones, want turnkey properties\nwithout the need to renovate, plus a flexible\nfloor plan, private outdoor space and\na walkable neighborhood, Hachiya adds. u)/P 55 0 R/K[3654 0 R]/Pg 27 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

