2948 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(\342\200\234In 2026, we\342\200\231re seeing a little more\ninventory come on the market from people\nwho were waiting for the right moment to\nsell,\342\200\235 he says. \342\200\234Now that the AI demand is\nhere and people feel more confident about\nan improving city, we expect sustainable\nstrength in the housing market.\342\200\235\nMumbai and\nNew Delhi, India\nThe momentum experienced in prime\nluxury property markets in India over the\npast year is likely to continue, particularly\nin iconic neighborhoods with limited\ninventory, according to Sudershan\nSharma, executive director, India\nSotheby\342\200\231s International Realty.\n\342\200\234With record sales and exceptional\nperformance in the ultra-luxury segment,\n2025 was a defining year,\342\200\235 Sharma says.\n\342\200\234We saw strong growth in both transaction\nvolume and prices, with overall values\nsurging over 40% in the last three years.\nHowever, inventory remains tight; the\nscarcity of \342\200\230trophy\342\200\231 properties in prime\nzones like Lutyens\342\200\231 Delhi and South\nMumbai has led to prices holding strong.\342\200\235\nLuxury homebuyers in India\ntraditionally come from legacy industrial\nfamilies, but Sharma says that new\ndemand is coming from younger\nhomebuyers, including startup founders,\nnext-generation entrepreneurs and C-suite\nexecutives monetizing their equity gains.)/P 55 0 R/K[3662 0 R]/Pg 28 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

2949 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(\342\200\234Many homebuyers are upgrading\ntheir primary residences for lifestyle value,\nand demand for second homes in hilly and\nbeach destinations is robust as an escape\nfrom urban intensity,\342\200\235 Sharma explains.\n\342\200\234Luxury real estate is viewed as a \342\200\230blue-\nchip asset class\342\200\231 for capital appreciation\nand efficient reinvestment of capital gains\nunder Indian tax laws.\342\200\235\nIndia\342\200\231s strong economic momentum\nand rising wealth levels along with interest-\nrate cuts and tax laws influence the luxury\nreal estate market there.\n\342\200\234The most desirable homes are\n\342\200\230experience-led\342\200\231 gated villas or high-rise\nproperties offering wellness, privacy and\ntop-of-the-line amenities,\342\200\235 Sharma says.\n\342\200\234We\342\200\231re also seeing exceptional demand\nfor\302\240second homes in drivable destinations\nfrom Mumbai. Simultaneously, the core\nmarkets in the financial capital remain\nrobust, particularly in iconic neighborhoods\nsuch as Nepean Sea Road, Malabar Hill\nand Worli, where the address itself carries\nan irreplaceable premium.\342\200\235\nLondon,\nUnited Kingdom\nHeadlines predicted that wealthy\nhomeowners would leave London after\nrecent tax law changes, but in 2025\nthe\302\240market performed better than in 2024\nin both sales volume and prices, according\nto Marcus O\342\200\231Brien, head of the family)/P 55 0 R/K[3663 0 R]/Pg 28 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

2950 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(office, United Kingdom Sotheby\342\200\231s\nInternational Realty.\n\342\200\234There was a degree of wealth\nmovement, and we saw some clients\nrelocate earlier in 2025, but this also\nreleased stock that hadn\342\200\231t come to market\nin two decades or more\342\200\224properties that\nare rarely traded and were met with\nstrong demand,\342\200\235 O\342\200\231Brien says.\n\342\200\234Turnkey properties with high-\ncaliber interior design continue to see\nthe strongest demand,\342\200\235 O\342\200\231Brien says.\n\342\200\234Buyers\302\240are prioritizing homes that are\nfully finished to a high aesthetic and\ntechnical standard. The demographic is\nalso shifting younger, to homebuyers in\ntheir 30s and early 40s, often founders\nor second-generation wealth, in the\nprime and super-prime brackets.\342\200\235\nWhile most purchases are for\nprimary residences, second home\nactivity has grown as the hybrid lifestyle\nbecomes more entrenched, he adds.\n\342\200\234The UK government\342\200\231s 2025-26 budget\nhas played a meaningful role influencing\nthe luxury housing market,\342\200\235 O\342\200\231Brien says.\n\342\200\234Clarity around property taxation and\nstamp duty\302\240thresholds has prompted\nsome homebuyers who were previously\non the sidelines to re-engage. Broader\neconomic stabilization, interest-rate\nexpectations and currency movements\n\342\200\224particularly sterling against the dollar\342\200\224\nare also shaping buying decisions.\342\200\235\nFor the rest of 2026, O\342\200\231Brien\nexpects\302\240more legacy properties to)/P 55 0 R/K[3664 0 R]/Pg 28 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

2951 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(\nthe demographic is shifting younger, to\nhomebuyers in their 30s and early 40s, often\nfounders or second-generation wealth.\n\n\n\n\nmarcus o\342\200\231brien, head of the family office, United Kingdom Sotheby\342\200\231s International Realty)/P 55 0 R/K[3665 0 R]/Pg 28 0 R/S/Figure>>
