2961 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(become\302\240available and to trade. \342\200\234If\neconomic policy remains predictable\nand interest rates ease, confidence will\ncontinue to\302\240build,\342\200\235 he says. \342\200\234Price growth\nis likely to\302\240be modest but positive, with\ncontinued competition for the highest-\nquality homes. As always, quality will\noutperform quantity.\342\200\235\nSingapore,\nRepublic of Singapore\nIts position as a mecca for UHNWIs as\nwell as its strong currency continue to\nbolster Singapore\342\200\231s reputation as a safe-\nhaven market, according to Nancy Tey,\nsenior associate vice president, List\nSotheby\342\200\231s International Realty, Singapore.\n\342\200\234Prices for non-landed properties\n[condominiums and apartments] were\nresilient throughout 2025, and some prime\ntrophy assets in Singapore achieved new\nbenchmark prices,\342\200\235 Tey says. \342\200\234Transaction\nvolumes were higher in 2025 because of\nmany condominium launches in the core\ncentral region.\342\200\235\nWhile properties with land are highly\ndesirable, they\342\200\231re also in short supply.\nThe\302\240properties that sell the fastest are\nnon-landed residences in Districts 9, 10\nand 11; \342\200\234Good Class Bungalows,\342\200\235 which\nare exclusive properties in designated\nareas with large lots; detached residences\nwith redevelopment potential; large-format\nunits with three or four bedrooms and\nprivacy; and move-in ready homes with\nhigh-quality finishes, Tey says. \342\200\234Most of\nour transactions are local homebuyers\nwho are Singaporean citizens or\npermanent residents in their 40s to 60s,\nbut we\342\200\231ve also seen a noticeable increase\nin younger homebuyers in tech and\nfinance, along with second-generation\nfamily wealth.\342\200\235 u)/P 55 0 R/K[3672 0 R]/Pg 29 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

