3002 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(The wealthiest homebuyers in the world are getting\nolder,\302\240and they\342\200\231re building homes to match their lifestyles.\nAs\302\240global populations live longer, the luxury real estate\nmarket is undergoing a fundamental transformation.\n\342\200\234Increasing life expectancy, coupled with affluent\nhomebuyers\342\200\231 desire to \342\200\230age in place,\342\200\231 is reshaping\neverything from home design and location choice to\nhow\302\240inventory circulates in prime real estate markets,\342\200\235\nsays Tammy\302\240Fahmi, senior vice president, global\nservicing and\302\240strategy, Sotheby\342\200\231s International Realty.\nThis aging millionaire boom, increasingly rooted\nin\302\240longevity and wellness economics, is creating new\nopportunities as well as challenges for luxury real estate.\nGlobal life expectancy at birth has risen significantly\nwithin the last century, from roughly 46 years in 1950\nto 73 years in 2023, according to a November 2025\nreport by Statista.1 By 2100, people around the world\nare expected to live to 82 years on average, with Europe and\nNorth America predicted to have even higher life\nexpectancies of\302\240around 90 years.\n\342\200\234Many buyers in their late 50s, 60s and 70s\nare\302\240not\302\240downsizing or moving into senior housing,\342\200\235\nsays\302\240Anna Sherrill, senior global real estate advisor,\nONE\302\240Sotheby\342\200\231s International Realty in Miami, Florida.\n\342\200\234Instead, they are purchasing or staying in homes that)/P 55 0 R/K[3698 0 R]/Pg 34 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

3003 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(allow them to live well\302\240for a very long time. These are\nclients who are highly focused on their health, longevity\nand quality of life.\342\200\235\nLongevity-Driven Economics\nThe growing emphasis on longevity is not merely\na lifestyle trend\342\200\224it\342\200\231s a major economic force that continues\nto gain momentum. UBS Global Wealth Management\nprojected in\302\240March 2025 that the worldwide longevity\nmarket, which encompasses health, wellness and aging-\nrelated spending, will grow from US$5.3 trillion in 2023\nto US$8 trillion by 2030, reflecting strong demographic\nand consumer shifts toward longer, healthier lives.2\nThis expansion reflects a broader macroeconomic\nshift: as populations age and life expectancy rises,\ndemand increases for the services, products and\nproperties that help maintain a higher quality of life\nwell\302\240into later years. The world\342\200\231s over-60 population\nis expected to double by 2050, surpassing two billion\npeople, UBS reported.\nReal estate sits squarely in the path of that spending.\nWithin the broader longevity economy, property has\nemerged as a quickly expanding segment. \342\200\234Wellness)/P 55 0 R/K[3699 0 R]/Pg 34 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

3004 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(Global Life Expectancy\nSource: \342\200\234Global Life Expectancy at Birth From 1950 to 2023, With Projections Until 2100,\342\200\235 Statista, November 28, 2025)/P 55 0 R/K[3700 0 R]/Pg 34 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

