3155 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(survey say aging in place is a growing concern among\nhomebuyers, and they are increasingly seeking built-in\nwellness features, such as:\n\nUniversal design elements that ensure accessibility\nas mobility changes over time, including a preference\nfor single-story residences and curbless bathrooms\nDirect contact with the outdoors, such as\non-property hiking or walking trails, encouraging\nregular exercise and prioritizing privacy and quiet\nenvironments, particularly water views.\nAir- and water-quality optimization, including\nadvanced filtration systems.\nSpa-like amenities, including sauna, steam, cold\nplunges, red-light therapy, massage rooms and\nfully equipped gyms.\nIntegrated tech and staff to support health\nmonitoring, mobility assistance and smart\nhome devices.\nHealthy dining options, such as juice bars and\naccess to private chefs, with an emphasis on\norganic products and plant-based options.\n\nThese features not only enhance quality of life but also\nsupport longevity goals\342\200\224making a home not just a place\nto live but a place to thrive in peace.\n\342\200\234Developers are responding to homebuyer demands\nby blending age-friendly design with access to clinical\nservices and urban mobility,\342\200\235 Fahmi explains. \342\200\234Branded\nresidences will become more in demand\342\200\224collaborations\nin the hospitality, fashion and automotive sectors\nindicate\302\240the direction of the luxury real estate market\ntoward service-rich living arrangements. Homes are\nbecoming hubs for care, connection and continuity\342\200\224\nwhere luxury is\302\240also defined by adaptability, wellness\nand\302\240community.\342\200\235\nWellness Communities\nBeyond individual homes, communities built around\nlongevity are gaining traction, and they differ from\ntraditional senior housing in many respects. High-\nend\302\240residential enclaves in Florida, Mexico and other\ncoastal\302\240regions increasingly integrate on-site medical\nprofessionals, wellness programming and preventive)/P 55 0 R/K[3767 0 R]/Pg 37 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

3156 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(care\302\240facilities, mirroring hospitality models but designed\nfor permanent residency.\n\342\200\234Many clients are looking for homes near the ocean\nor\302\240directly on the water, with an emphasis on natural\nlight,\302\240walkability and an overall healthy lifestyle,\342\200\235 Sherrill\nsays. \342\200\234I\302\240recently closed on a residence at the Estates\nat\302\240Acqualina in Sunny Isles Beach, Florida, where the\namenities were a key reason for the purchase. Many newer\nluxury buildings now feel more like living in a resort. In\naddition, I have represented homebuyers at the St. Regis\nResidences in Miami, Florida, which offers more than\n50,000 square feet of spa-like amenities, including cold\nplunges, saunas, a salt spa room and a state-of-the-art\nfitness center with private trainers, yoga, Pilates and\nspinning. This level of wellness is becoming standard\nin newer construction.\342\200\235\nSuch communities draw homebuyers seeking\nboth a luxury lifestyle and longevity. According to\nGiovana Saldivar, senior global real estate advisor,\nRiviera Maya Sotheby\342\200\231s International Realty in Mexico,\nthese developments reflect a strategic recognition\nthat wealthy homebuyers increasingly view their\nhomes and communities as primary wellness assets.\n\342\200\234There is a conscious search for peace, silence\nand\302\240a\302\240connection with the environment, rather than\nan\302\240active social or urban life,\342\200\235 Saldivar explains. \342\200\234For\nthis reason, villas located in the Sian Ka\342\200\231an Biosphere\nReserve in Quintana Roo, Mexico, generate a very\nstrong interest in\302\240this homebuyer profile. This type\nof property offers them exactly what they are looking\nfor: a protected natural setting, views, absolute privacy and\na high level of\302\240security.\342\200\235\nSalvidar\342\200\231s clients focus their purchasing decisions\non two key factors: security and peace. \342\200\234In the context\nof the Riviera Maya, Mexico, these values translate\nalmost immediately and indisputably into Sian Ka\342\200\231an\342\200\224\na destination that represents well-being, prestige and\na quality of life designed for the long term,\342\200\235 she says.\nLessons From the Blue Zones\nResearch into regions where longevity is the norm,\ncommonly referred to as \342\200\234Blue Zones,\342\200\235 provides additional\ninsights on the topic. The term was first coined in 2005 by\nNational Geographic explorer Dan Buettner to describe\nregions with above-average concentrations of people who\nlive to be 100 years old and who often report lower rates\nof chronic disease than elsewhere.4 As identified by the\nNational Library of Medicine in 2016, the original five Blue\nZones are Ikaria, Greece; Okinawa, Japan; Sardinia, Italy; u)/P 55 0 R/K[3780 0 R]/Pg 37 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

