3177 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(Future-Proofing\nfor\302\240Younger Buyers\nThe longevity conversation in luxury real\nestate isn\342\200\231t only happening among older\nhomebuyers\342\200\224it\342\200\231s starting decades earlier\nthan you might expect. Luxury real estate\nprofessionals across global prime markets\nare reporting a notable demographic shift\namong buyers\342\200\224namely, an increase of\nbuyers in their 30s and 40s. While ultra-\nluxury ownership was once dominated by\nolder, legacy-wealth buyers, today\342\200\231s property\nmarket reflects the growing influence of\nyounger high-net-worth individuals.\nAccording to the 2026 Mid-Year\nSotheby\342\200\231s International Realty agent\nsurvey, the biggest surge in homebuyers\nwere millennials \(those born between\n1981 and 1996\), with 55% of respondents\nfrom around the world seeing an uptick of\nhomebuyers from this generation in their\nreal estate markets, particularly those\ndealing in properties worth US$5 million)/P 55 0 R/K[3794 0 R]/Pg 39 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

3178 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(or more. \342\200\234It\342\200\231s incredible to see how\nmany buyers in their 30s and 40s are\npurchasing properties in the US$7 million\nto US$25 million range,\342\200\235 says Anna Sherrill,\nsenior global real estate advisor, ONE\nSotheby\342\200\231s International Realty in Miami,\nFlorida. \342\200\234Some have inherited wealth,\nwhile others are entrepreneurs in tech,\nfinance, crypto or other businesses they\nbuilt at a young age.\342\200\235 Others represent\nthe next generation of family wealth,\nbenefiting from intergenerational transfers\nthat are occurring sooner and in larger\namounts, she adds.\n\342\200\234What is interesting is that many\nyounger buyers are already very focused\non health, longevity and anti-aging,\342\200\235\nSherrill continues. In a March 2025 report,\nUBS notes that demographic shifts, rising\nlife expectancy and an accelerating transfer\nof wealth between generations\nare key drivers of changing homebuyer\nbehavior.6 Affluent households are\nplanning for longer, healthier lives and\nentering the luxury real estate market\342\200\224)/P 55 0 R/K[3795 0 R]/Pg 39 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

3179 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(not as late-career consolidators but\nas long-term lifestyle planners.\n\342\200\234While older homebuyers tend\nto prioritize continuity, comfort and\nhealthcare access, younger homebuyers\nfocus on flexibility and performance\342\200\224both\nlifestyle-driven and investment-oriented. Yet\nthe convergence is unmistakable,\342\200\235\nsays Elena Araya Call\303\255s, country manager,\nCosta Rica Sotheby\342\200\231s International Realty.\nUnlike older buyers who may retrofit\nproperties later in order to age in place,\nyounger buyers increasingly want homes\nthat are \342\200\234future-proofed\342\200\235 from the start\342\200\224\nthat is, designed to support their well-being\nover decades.\n\342\200\234They expect high-level wellness\namenities, strong design, privacy, and\nturnkey living from the beginning,\342\200\235 says\nSherrill. \342\200\234Compared to older buyers, they\ntend to be more global, more design-driven\nand less willing to compromise. They are\nbuying with a long-term lifestyle in mind,\neven if they do not plan to live in the home\nfull-time right away.\342\200\235 \342\226\240)/P 55 0 R/K[3796 0 R]/Pg 39 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

