3213 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(New York, New York\n\342\200\234Betting against New York City is never a good idea,\342\200\235 says\nWendy Arriz, senior global real estate advisor, Sotheby\342\200\231s\nInternational Realty - East Side Manhattan Brokerage.\n\342\200\234It has a one-of-a-kind spirit that can\342\200\231t be beat.\342\200\235\nIn the past 25 years, New York has survived\na terrorist attack, a global financial crisis that began on\nWall Street and a pandemic that led to a rise in remote\nand hybrid work, reshaping the commercial real estate\nlandscape across the city, and sending many residents\nto less-dense locations with lower costs of living. Despite\nthat not-so-distant history, New York is growing, with a 4%\nyear-on-year increase in sales in the first quarter of 2026,\ngenerating US$6.2 billion in total sales, according to real\nestate market data published in April 2026 by Sotheby\342\200\231s\nInternational Realty - New York City Brokerages.4\n\342\200\234There is an endless supply of people who are\nNew York enthusiasts,\342\200\235 says Arriz, who is based on the\nUpper East Side. \342\200\234That\342\200\231s where the energy comes\nfrom\342\200\224really dynamic people live here, because of the\nbusiness community and the unrivaled arts. For someone\nwho appreciates that and wants that, there\342\200\231s no other place\nthat competes.\342\200\235 Housing sales activity has increased\nsince 2019, according to Arriz. However, despite\neye-popping deals for ultra-luxury residences, overall prices\nhave been relatively stable over the past decade.\n\342\200\234New York is undervalued. Prices have been flat\nfor\302\240a\302\240long time and the rest of the nation has had a lot\nof\302\240appreciation that we haven\342\200\231t seen,\342\200\235 she explains. For\nexample, the median price of a Manhattan residence in\n2016 was $1.15 million, The New York Times reported\nin January 2016,5 while almost a decade later, in 2025, it\nwas $1.28 million, according to Sotheby\342\200\231s International\nRealty - New York City Brokerages data.6 \342\200\234At some\npoint we\342\200\231re likely going to see more appreciation,\342\200\235 Arriz\ncontinues, \342\200\234so\302\240I think there\342\200\231s a value play in investing\nin New York property right now.\342\200\235)/P 55 0 R/K[3819 0 R]/Pg 43 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

3214 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(Buyers are looking for \342\200\234move-in-ready or move-in-\nready-ish\342\200\235 units, although they are willing to do a slight\n\342\200\234nip and tuck\342\200\235 to a home, such as swapping out the\ncountertops or renovating a bathroom, Arriz adds.\nHowever, a value-driven buyer may choose to do the\nwork\302\240after purchasing, since they may reap a healthy\nreturn on investment \(ROI\).\nNew York City\342\200\231s property market, like many others,\nis\302\240also driven by a lack of inventory. Last year, city officials\npaved the way for tens of thousands of new homes, many\naffordable, to be built in Manhattan and Queens, which\nwill take pressure off other areas of the real estate market.\nRecently, Arriz noticed a resurgence of luxury buyers\nmoving to Fifth or Park Avenue co-ops for perceived access\nto schools, wider streets and a stronger sense\nof neighborhood. Other buyers will pay a premium for\na brand-new development and are less sensitive to\nwhat neighborhood they live in, preferring to \342\200\234follow\nthe product,\342\200\235 Arriz explains.\nThat means the outer edges of Manhattan on First,\nSecond or Third Avenues; Long Island City; Queens or\nthe Financial District and Battery Park City, which are not\ntraditionally residential areas but saw a\302\24020% jump in sales\nin 2025, according to Sotheby\342\200\231s International Realty data.\n\342\200\234They don\342\200\231t seem to mind living there as much as previous\ngenerations,\342\200\235 she says. The\302\240expansion of the city\342\200\231s subway\nsystem to Second Avenue on the Upper East Side in 2017\nno doubt eased the\302\240way for some, Arriz adds.\nMeanwhile, headlines regarding a proposed tax\non second homes in New York City valued at $5 million\nor more7 have given the luxury property market pause.\n\342\200\234Many are waiting to see how this proposal unfolds,\342\200\235\nArriz says. \342\200\234It is not the first time real estate professionals\nhave had to navigate a significant shift in the marketplace.\342\200\235\nShe also sees no signs of the real estate market slowing\ndown, especially with Wall Street performing so well.\n\342\200\234Barring some unforeseen situation, we expect the\nrest of 2026 to\302\240be a strong year,\342\200\235 she says. u)/P 55 0 R/K[3820 0 R]/Pg 43 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

