3227 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(Los Angeles, California\nNatural beauty and warm weather are two of the biggest\nattractions of living in Los Angeles, according to Ernie\nCarswell, senior global real estate advisor, Sotheby\342\200\231s\nInternational Realty - Beverly Hills Brokerage.\n\342\200\234We just have it so good out here,\342\200\235 he says. \342\200\234We\342\200\231re\nconstantly saying how blessed we are with the weather\nor\302\240with the beauty of the backdrop, the mountains down\nto\302\240the ocean. It\342\200\231s something that we love and we don\342\200\231t\nwant to let go of.\342\200\235\nNot everyone agrees, Carswell concedes, as\nthere have been some departures of high-net-worth\nindividuals from California for no-income-tax states\nlike Texas and Florida. Still, home prices remain \342\200\234well\nabove the levels in\302\2402019, in spite of the cooling off\nwe\342\200\231ve had since 2024,\342\200\235 he says.\nThis has been due to two upheavals in the local\nproperty market, Carswell notes. The first involved the\naddition of the so-called mansion tax, or the United to\nHouse LA Act, which took effect in April 2023. It\302\240adds\na 4% levy for properties priced at US$5.3 million and\nup and a 5.5% surcharge for properties conveyed at\nUS$10.6 million or more, according to the city\342\200\231s Office\nof Finance.8 \342\200\234It doubled the cost of selling a home if you\nown a luxury property,\342\200\235 he\302\240explains.\nThen the city was devastated by major fires in\nJanuary 2025. Around 17,000 homes in the Altadena\nand Pacific Palisades neighborhoods were destroyed,\naccording to a\302\240January 2026 report by Columbia\nUniversity\342\200\231s Climate School.9 The recovery has been faster\nin the more affluent Pacific Palisades area, while Altadena\nis still reeling and recovery has been slow, Carswell says.)/P 55 0 R/K[3829 0 R]/Pg 44 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

3228 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(\nthere\342\200\231s a spirit in los Angeles of ultimate\nfreedom that you don\342\200\231t want to give up.\nthat\342\200\231s why this city keeps rebounding.\n\n)/P 55 0 R/K[3830 0 R]/Pg 44 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

3229 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(ernie carswell, senior global real estate advisor, Sotheby\342\200\231s International Realty - Beverly Hills Brokerage)/P 55 0 R/K[3831 0 R]/Pg 44 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

3230 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt()/P 55 0 R/K[2130 0 R]/Pg 44 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

3231 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/Alt(In addition, fires over the last decade have also\nincreased insurance costs in the most susceptible\nareas. The owner of a four-bedroom home in Brentwood,\nwhich neighbors Pacific Palisades, could now pay\nUS$120,000 a year in insurance, up from around\nUS$20,000 a decade ago. Brentwood has also seen\nhouse\302\240prices jump more than 50% in the past year as\nmany displaced families look to stay local, he notes.\nBut the city\342\200\231s housing options are very diverse.\n\342\200\234Los\302\240Angeles is a patchwork quilt of values, styles,\ntopography and even fire liabilities,\342\200\235 Carswell says. \342\200\234\nThere\342\200\231s a lot to consider.\342\200\235\nAcross the Los Angeles metro area, prices were\nup 0.4% in March 2026 compared to the same time\nthe year before, according to data published by the\nCalifornia Association of Realtors in April 2026.10\nHomes continue to appreciate, with property investors\nmaking money despite\302\240added taxes or fees. California\nalso has one of\302\240the\302\240strongest economies in the world,\nranking as the\302\240fourth-largest according to data released\nby the International Monetary Fund and the U.S. Bureau\nof\302\240Economic Analysis in April 2025.11\nSomething less concrete keeps Angelenos in place.\n\342\200\234There\342\200\231s a spirit in Los Angeles of ultimate freedom.\nAnd once you have it, you don\342\200\231t want to give it up,\342\200\235\nCarswell says. \342\200\234That\342\200\231s why this city keeps rebounding.\342\200\235\nMilan, Italy\nLong a business and industrial hub, as well as the financial\nand economic capital of Italy, Milan\342\200\231s transition into\na global city has accelerated in the past two decades.\nStarting in 2005, long-abandoned railyards in Porta u)/P 55 0 R/K[3832 0 R]/Pg 44 0 R/S/Figure>>
endobj

